1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:09,680
A simple shape, a span across space and time, and the single most influential form in Western

2
00:00:09,681 --> 00:00:12,938
civilization, perfected by the ancient Romans.

3
00:00:12,939 --> 00:00:16,480
It spans great rivers, takes us deep into the sea,

4
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:19,311
and will enable us to live on other worlds.

5
00:00:19,312 --> 00:00:21,880
It's the basic building block in human history,

6
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:25,240
finding its way into millions of structures for millennia.

7
00:00:25,241 --> 00:00:32,240
Now, The Arch on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:44,240 --> 00:00:45,240
The Arch.

9
00:00:45,241 --> 00:00:52,240
In its many forms, this uniquely adaptable and robust shape has dominated the Western world

10
00:00:52,241 --> 00:00:57,117
world since its invention over 2,000 years ago.

11
00:00:57,118 --> 00:01:01,060
Its characteristics, able to span long

12
00:01:01,061 --> 00:01:08,740
stretches, carry incredible loads, and withstand the ravages of time, all while being composed

13
00:01:08,741 --> 00:01:16,040
of the simplest materials, make it the most enduring building form known to man.

14
00:01:16,041 --> 00:01:22,220
The idea behind an arch is that it channels the force that the structure needs to support

15
00:01:22,221 --> 00:01:27,460
in a way that is most efficient in terms of the way it uses the material.

16
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:31,400
No other shape comes close to the arch and its relatives.

17
00:01:32,040 --> 00:01:35,880
The vault, cylinder, and dome for efficiency.

18
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:41,500
But there was a time when such structures were unknown.

19
00:01:43,900 --> 00:01:47,280
To imagine that you were alive 2,000 years ago

20
00:01:47,281 --> 00:01:51,380
and you wanted to build a bridge across a relatively wide stream,

21
00:01:51,381 --> 00:01:53,900
your choices weren't very many.

22
00:01:54,680 --> 00:01:57,300
You could fell a tree if the stream was narrow,

23
00:01:57,660 --> 00:02:00,400
and you could build a bridge by walking across the tree.

24
00:02:00,920 --> 00:02:03,400
The tree would then be what engineers call a beam.

25
00:02:05,060 --> 00:02:08,160
The simple idea, the beam,

26
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:11,560
had been in use since at least 12,000 B.C.

27
00:02:11,561 --> 00:02:15,420
at Stonehenge on the windswept plains of southern Britain.

28
00:02:15,421 --> 00:02:22,420
Beams, or lintels, spanned moderately spaced pillars in this ancient structure.

29
00:02:22,421 --> 00:02:29,420
The Greeks later used this method of construction for temples.

30
00:02:29,421 --> 00:02:38,420
It was practical and could be beautiful, but was limited to the width that a wood or stone beam could span before it collapsed.

31
00:02:38,421 --> 00:02:41,420
before it collapsed.

32
00:02:41,421 --> 00:02:45,420
But somewhere, sometime in the ancient world,

33
00:02:45,421 --> 00:02:49,420
someone realized that there was a better way to build grand,

34
00:02:49,421 --> 00:02:52,420
yet practical structures.

35
00:02:52,421 --> 00:02:56,420
Unfortunately, building materials in the ancient world had limitations,

36
00:02:56,421 --> 00:02:59,420
forcing early architects to be creative.

37
00:02:59,421 --> 00:03:04,420
The constraint that the Romans had to operate under was the availability of materials.

38
00:03:04,421 --> 00:03:07,420
You can't build beams out of blocks of rock.

39
00:03:07,421 --> 00:03:13,420
You could build beams out of timber, that was available to the Romans, but again, you were limited to very short spans.

40
00:03:13,421 --> 00:03:22,420
So by necessity, they had to discover a new structural form, a shape, which would generate only compressive forces

41
00:03:22,421 --> 00:03:25,420
for which both rock and concrete were ideal materials.

42
00:03:25,421 --> 00:03:32,420
And, aha, the innovativeness of Roman engineers that discovered that the arch was the ideal form for that time,

43
00:03:32,421 --> 00:03:35,420
and still is an ideal form for our time.

44
00:03:37,420 --> 00:03:42,420
They took an old and simple idea and built a civilization upon it.

45
00:03:43,420 --> 00:03:46,420
But what really makes this form so wondrous?

46
00:03:48,420 --> 00:03:55,420
The simple idea of the hanging chain really illustrates all of the main structural principles of an arch.

47
00:03:55,421 --> 00:03:59,420
And it's much easier to visualize. So here we have a force intention,

48
00:03:59,421 --> 00:04:03,420
and if you could stand on your head or freeze this and turn it over,

49
00:04:03,421 --> 00:04:08,000
You could picture exactly what's happening for the forces in an arch.

50
00:04:08,360 --> 00:04:13,180
And again, the key idea is that this string, it never makes a mistake.

51
00:04:13,580 --> 00:04:17,800
It never lies and is always finding the pure tension form

52
00:04:17,801 --> 00:04:19,800
because the string can only carry tension.

53
00:04:20,440 --> 00:04:24,320
In the same way, a masonry structure is a material that doesn't lie

54
00:04:24,321 --> 00:04:25,500
and can only carry compression.

55
00:04:25,960 --> 00:04:30,620
The arch can carry enormous weight and is purely a compression structure.

56
00:04:30,621 --> 00:04:33,620
that is, it can withstand pressure.

57
00:04:33,621 --> 00:04:38,620
The more force that's applied, the more compressed the arch becomes.

58
00:04:38,621 --> 00:04:44,620
On the other hand, if you apply tension to it, it loses its strength rapidly.

59
00:04:44,621 --> 00:04:49,620
As the legs of the arch begin to spread, the arch can collapse.

60
00:04:49,621 --> 00:04:58,620
The secret of stability in arch design is to contain the forces, or the line of thrust as it's called, within the arch.

61
00:04:58,621 --> 00:05:06,660
As we move supports around, the arch can adjust to these different movements and can adapt with new internal forces.

62
00:05:07,060 --> 00:05:12,400
And the key, really, or the magic is that they can carry so many different possible loadings.

63
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:16,700
And as the ground moves, the arch quite happily adjusts to it.

64
00:05:16,940 --> 00:05:20,860
It's in equilibrium. It stands. We can find a line of forces through one.

65
00:05:21,760 --> 00:05:25,680
As long as the line of force is contained inside the structure of the arch,

66
00:05:25,681 --> 00:05:28,780
or terminates at the arch supports,

67
00:05:29,440 --> 00:05:31,720
it can stand practically forever.

68
00:05:32,900 --> 00:05:35,700
But if the line of force moves outside the arch

69
00:05:35,701 --> 00:05:38,960
because of increased loads or changes in shape,

70
00:05:39,660 --> 00:05:40,620
it will fail.

71
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:44,220
The Romans understood this.

72
00:05:45,180 --> 00:05:47,860
It's a tribute to the fact that the Roman engineers knew about

73
00:05:47,861 --> 00:05:50,640
how their structures could redistribute forces

74
00:05:50,641 --> 00:05:52,380
even after they become damaged.

75
00:05:52,381 --> 00:05:56,380
Arches come in all sizes, shapes and styles.

76
00:05:56,381 --> 00:06:00,380
The classic Romanesque arch is a semicircle,

77
00:06:00,381 --> 00:06:04,380
built of stone or concrete.

78
00:06:04,381 --> 00:06:08,380
The Gothic arch from the 12th to 15th centuries has a pointed crown,

79
00:06:08,381 --> 00:06:12,380
allowing for more open spaces.

80
00:06:12,381 --> 00:06:16,380
Other arches include the basket-handle arch, popular in Britain,

81
00:06:16,381 --> 00:06:20,380
which allowed a very broad opening.

82
00:06:20,381 --> 00:06:27,580
And in the east, overextended or Moorish arches appeared, with less structurally sound but

83
00:06:27,581 --> 00:06:30,960
unique-looking forms.

84
00:06:30,961 --> 00:06:39,360
Although an arch's primary purpose is to support a load, its shape invited artistic innovations.

85
00:06:39,361 --> 00:06:45,120
As arch design progressed, the use of stone became a highly refined craft.

86
00:06:45,121 --> 00:06:50,300
The placement of stones, and especially the keystone, became a personal statement of the

87
00:06:50,301 --> 00:06:51,300
designer.

88
00:06:51,301 --> 00:06:58,040
A keystone is the last stone placed in a traditional arch.

89
00:06:58,041 --> 00:07:02,120
It's the stone that locks the arch into place.

90
00:07:02,121 --> 00:07:05,280
Keystones were traditionally made larger and more important looking than the other

91
00:07:05,281 --> 00:07:08,200
boussoirs or arch stones.

92
00:07:08,201 --> 00:07:12,600
But surprisingly, this was largely a cosmetic choice.

93
00:07:12,601 --> 00:07:17,220
You hear a lot of talk about the keystone in an arch being this most critical piece,

94
00:07:17,221 --> 00:07:18,880
this most special piece.

95
00:07:18,881 --> 00:07:23,220
The keystone has the smallest amount of force, yet it gets all of this attention.

96
00:07:23,221 --> 00:07:24,680
It's kind of a paradox.

97
00:07:24,681 --> 00:07:29,180
You'd expect to have more force at the middle, but the smallest force is at the middle.

98
00:07:29,181 --> 00:07:33,200
And therefore a keystone does not actually carry very much force.

99
00:07:33,201 --> 00:07:38,800
If you have an odd number of vussoirs, they're called the pieces of an arch, then there is

100
00:07:38,801 --> 00:07:41,300
an odd one in the middle, which is the keystone.

101
00:07:41,301 --> 00:07:45,720
If however you make the arch out of an even number, then there is no keystone.

102
00:07:45,721 --> 00:07:53,380
But being the last stone in the arch, the keystone does have one very special purpose.

103
00:07:53,381 --> 00:07:59,100
Without it, the arch will fall down.

104
00:07:59,101 --> 00:08:02,920
The ancient Romans were the first to make structural use of the arch in large buildings

105
00:08:02,921 --> 00:08:09,720
and bridges, and solved some of the great building problems of their day.

106
00:08:09,721 --> 00:08:13,520
but other obstacles remain.

107
00:08:13,521 --> 00:08:18,380
Another great challenge that the Roman engineers faced was how do you carry water over long

108
00:08:18,381 --> 00:08:21,040
distances and particularly over valleys.

109
00:08:21,041 --> 00:08:24,700
And you see these enormous structures, several tiers high.

110
00:08:24,701 --> 00:08:29,080
And in their aqueducts they typically began with larger spans at the bottom and went to

111
00:08:29,081 --> 00:08:33,800
smaller and smaller spans at the top to reduce the amount of material at the top.

112
00:08:33,801 --> 00:08:38,800
And also because a smaller arch is more stable than a longer span arch.

113
00:08:38,801 --> 00:08:43,380
They're very stable, and then they load the bigger arches near the base of the aqueduct,

114
00:08:43,381 --> 00:08:48,660
and those arches are made stable by the surcharge of all of the arches above them.

115
00:08:49,320 --> 00:08:55,200
The Pont du Gard aqueduct in France is almost 1,000 feet long and 160 feet high,

116
00:08:55,420 --> 00:09:01,860
with arches on the bottom level ranging from 50 to 70 feet wide and 65 feet high.

117
00:09:02,760 --> 00:09:07,060
The uppermost arches are only 10 feet wide and 28 feet high.

118
00:09:07,061 --> 00:09:13,820
The purpose of an arch is twofold, to change direction of applied force, number one, and

119
00:09:13,821 --> 00:09:18,880
number two, to make sure that the forces that are now going in a different direction are

120
00:09:18,881 --> 00:09:19,680
in compression.

121
00:09:20,780 --> 00:09:25,320
These astounding structures were a stunning testament to the ingenuity of their designers.

122
00:09:26,660 --> 00:09:30,240
But there was much more to come from the basic design of the aqueduct.

123
00:09:30,900 --> 00:09:36,140
I think of the Colosseum kind of as a Roman aqueduct that's folded onto itself to enclose

124
00:09:36,141 --> 00:09:39,780
space.

125
00:09:39,781 --> 00:09:44,400
It was the arena of the emperor and the citizens of Rome.

126
00:09:44,401 --> 00:09:49,440
Over 600 feet across at its widest point and 160 feet high,

127
00:09:49,441 --> 00:09:52,700
the Colosseum was home to myriad events.

128
00:09:52,701 --> 00:09:55,660
It allowed up to 75,000 spectators

129
00:09:55,661 --> 00:09:57,640
to watch great gladiatorial battles

130
00:09:57,641 --> 00:09:58,940
in architectural splendor.

131
00:10:01,940 --> 00:10:06,120
The use of concrete is perhaps the most exciting aspect

132
00:10:06,121 --> 00:10:08,780
of Roman building technology.

133
00:10:08,781 --> 00:10:10,460
The arch is a structural form.

134
00:10:10,461 --> 00:10:13,460
It would work with concrete, or it would work with rock

135
00:10:13,461 --> 00:10:18,200
or masonry, but the Romans took the arch to another level.

136
00:10:18,201 --> 00:10:20,500
The Roman invention and use of concrete

137
00:10:20,501 --> 00:10:23,200
for construction led to the development

138
00:10:23,201 --> 00:10:27,200
of another use of the arch, the barrel vault.

139
00:10:27,201 --> 00:10:29,640
So instead of a single arch now, you have a room

140
00:10:29,641 --> 00:10:32,380
with a series of arches, and so that's three-dimensional.

141
00:10:32,381 --> 00:10:33,420
It's a vault.

142
00:10:33,421 --> 00:10:34,820
But the Romans went way beyond that.

143
00:10:34,821 --> 00:10:40,820
They also took arches and they turned some arches 90 degrees to the first and made intersecting

144
00:10:40,821 --> 00:10:41,820
arches.

145
00:10:41,821 --> 00:10:44,880
So it's as if you took two barrel vaults and intersected them.

146
00:10:44,881 --> 00:10:49,300
And in the middle you have a big open area that's a growing vault.

147
00:10:49,301 --> 00:10:53,000
That's a clear Roman innovation.

148
00:10:53,001 --> 00:10:58,300
Impressive though it is, the Colosseum was just a precursor for more remarkable structures

149
00:10:58,301 --> 00:11:01,020
to come.

150
00:11:01,021 --> 00:11:05,289
across Rome. The Pantheon was built in 125 A.D.

151
00:11:05,290 --> 00:11:08,740
This innovative building would set the

152
00:11:08,741 --> 00:11:10,420
standard for centuries to come.

153
00:11:11,700 --> 00:11:17,020
One sees structures like the Pantheon, and the most shocking, breathtaking aspect of

154
00:11:17,021 --> 00:11:21,138
walking into a structure like that is open space.

155
00:11:21,139 --> 00:11:24,080
What domes afford is interior space

156
00:11:24,081 --> 00:11:26,278
unencumbered by column support.

157
00:11:26,279 --> 00:11:30,320
The columns, of course, or the walls are on the periphery

158
00:11:30,321 --> 00:11:35,760
of the dome, and they're transmitting all the compression force from the dome down through

159
00:11:35,761 --> 00:11:37,880
the walls into the foundation.

160
00:11:37,881 --> 00:11:43,140
The roof of the Pantheon was the world's first large dome.

161
00:11:43,141 --> 00:11:46,740
It was an unprecedented accomplishment.

162
00:11:46,741 --> 00:11:50,880
You could think of a dome as a series of arches that are all leaning on each other in the

163
00:11:50,881 --> 00:11:52,240
middle.

164
00:11:52,241 --> 00:11:58,120
But in addition to that, a dome acts as a series of hoops that surround the dome.

165
00:11:58,121 --> 00:12:05,120
So what prevents the dome from falling inward, there are arches in plan that are really circles

166
00:12:05,121 --> 00:12:10,120
that also can carry load in each ring of the dome as you build up.

167
00:12:10,121 --> 00:12:16,120
The Pantheon has a traditional temple entrance, which opens to the great dome-covered interior.

168
00:12:16,121 --> 00:12:19,120
But there's a surprise once inside.

169
00:12:19,121 --> 00:12:24,120
In the exact center of the dome, there's a hole that brings in light.

170
00:12:24,121 --> 00:12:28,560
So you have all of these arches that are leaning together to form the dome,

171
00:12:28,561 --> 00:12:31,780
and exactly at the center where they all want to lean together,

172
00:12:32,480 --> 00:12:38,040
Roman engineers take away the material and build a hole in the middle of the dome.

173
00:12:38,560 --> 00:12:42,160
The 30-foot-wide hole in the dome center, called an oculus,

174
00:12:42,720 --> 00:12:45,180
is a huge compression ring made of stone,

175
00:12:45,560 --> 00:12:48,360
which in effect is a series of arches forming a circle.

176
00:12:49,340 --> 00:12:52,600
The horizontal forces of the dome, which keep the structure stable,

177
00:12:52,601 --> 00:13:01,540
converge at the top. It was a daring move by the Roman engineers. The Pantheon was

178
00:13:01,541 --> 00:13:06,760
an amazing achievement, but after the fall of Rome, builders would find other

179
00:13:06,761 --> 00:13:11,100
spectacular uses for the arch.

180
00:13:13,640 --> 00:13:19,840
The Colosseum originally had 80 arches in each exterior level, totaling 240

181
00:13:19,841 --> 00:13:32,420
arches above ground alone.

182
00:13:32,421 --> 00:13:36,600
By 476 A.D., the Roman Empire had fallen.

183
00:13:36,601 --> 00:13:41,920
Later, medieval church builders would take up the use of the arch, building primarily

184
00:13:41,921 --> 00:13:44,820
with stone.

185
00:13:44,821 --> 00:13:49,060
would not reappear for almost 1800 years.

186
00:13:49,061 --> 00:13:51,700
But the designers of the Gothic era used the arch

187
00:13:51,701 --> 00:13:54,000
in ways the Romans could have only dreamed.

188
00:13:57,300 --> 00:14:00,800
The biggest advancement during the Gothic era

189
00:14:00,801 --> 00:14:06,800
was getting away from the barrel-type vault

190
00:14:06,801 --> 00:14:10,480
and using more of an elliptical arch or a pointed arch.

191
00:14:10,481 --> 00:14:13,940
So from a visual perspective, it allowed the arch

192
00:14:13,941 --> 00:14:14,980
to be much taller.

193
00:14:15,680 --> 00:14:20,360
And the goal of the master builders during the Gothic era

194
00:14:20,361 --> 00:14:22,180
was to try to bring in as much light

195
00:14:22,181 --> 00:14:24,440
to the interior of the cathedrals as they could.

196
00:14:24,820 --> 00:14:27,840
So the taller they could make the arches relative to the width,

197
00:14:28,280 --> 00:14:30,640
they were able to introduce far more area window.

198
00:14:31,980 --> 00:14:33,880
The era of the Gothic cathedral

199
00:14:33,881 --> 00:14:36,840
also saw the use of less massive design elements.

200
00:14:38,080 --> 00:14:40,420
Continuing to strive for higher and grander,

201
00:14:40,421 --> 00:14:47,420
Church builders developed new techniques to maximize their uses of the arch.

202
00:14:47,421 --> 00:14:57,420
Gothic cathedrals such as Notre Dame in Paris are lasting examples of the incredibly complex workmanship of the master builders of the time.

203
00:14:57,421 --> 00:15:02,420
Cathedral ceilings reached heights of up to 150 feet.

204
00:15:02,421 --> 00:15:09,420
As these arches soared ever higher, materials were used more sparingly to allow for this vertical growth.

205
00:15:09,421 --> 00:15:13,100
resulting in thinner walls, pillars, and vaults.

206
00:15:14,900 --> 00:15:16,780
But with this increase in height,

207
00:15:17,260 --> 00:15:20,280
intense forces were exerted by the soaring stone arches

208
00:15:20,281 --> 00:15:22,120
and had to go somewhere.

209
00:15:23,220 --> 00:15:25,840
The Gothic designers came up with an ingenious solution.

210
00:15:27,060 --> 00:15:30,420
You still have to get the thrust of the arch down to the ground.

211
00:15:30,920 --> 00:15:32,020
So how do you do that?

212
00:15:32,120 --> 00:15:34,180
That's the real challenge of Gothic construction.

213
00:15:34,181 --> 00:15:38,220
And this is where the really ingenious breakthrough comes

214
00:15:38,221 --> 00:15:46,400
of building flying buttresses, which are exterior, elevated, half arches, really, that carry

215
00:15:46,401 --> 00:15:49,840
the flow of forces from the main vault down to the ground.

216
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:56,280
So as the arched ceilings grew wider and taller, the sides of the building wanted to spread.

217
00:15:57,360 --> 00:16:02,620
The use of flying buttresses provided a rock-solid method of reinforcement for the thin cathedral

218
00:16:02,621 --> 00:16:03,321
walls.

219
00:16:04,380 --> 00:16:06,880
You can think of it as half of a Gothic arch.

220
00:16:06,881 --> 00:16:08,900
so a Gothic arch with a point at the middle,

221
00:16:09,440 --> 00:16:11,860
if you take those two sides and separate them,

222
00:16:12,380 --> 00:16:13,880
they each want to lean inward,

223
00:16:14,340 --> 00:16:16,320
and so they can help to resist the thrust

224
00:16:16,321 --> 00:16:19,540
from the central nave that acts between those two flying arches.

225
00:16:20,640 --> 00:16:23,320
Flying buttresses became a very reliable method

226
00:16:23,321 --> 00:16:24,700
of building grand structures.

227
00:16:26,060 --> 00:16:28,180
But this method required precision.

228
00:16:29,320 --> 00:16:31,660
In some buildings, such as Selby Abbey in England,

229
00:16:32,600 --> 00:16:34,180
arches have deformed over time

230
00:16:34,181 --> 00:16:36,100
due to incorrect placement of buttresses.

231
00:16:36,880 --> 00:16:43,880
The flying buttress was perhaps the greatest inspiration of the Gothic era.

232
00:16:43,881 --> 00:16:50,660
After the Gothic period, as the Renaissance bloomed, the dome reemerged from its Roman

233
00:16:50,661 --> 00:16:54,520
origins to new and spectacular uses.

234
00:16:54,521 --> 00:16:55,649
In 1626, St.

235
00:16:55,650 --> 00:17:02,520
Peter's Basilica was completed just a few miles from its predecessor, the

236
00:17:02,521 --> 00:17:05,000
Pantheon.

237
00:17:05,001 --> 00:17:08,760
The new dome was 138 feet wide.

238
00:17:08,761 --> 00:17:13,460
Fable has it that the designers had intended it to be smaller than the Pantheon, out of

239
00:17:13,461 --> 00:17:16,460
respect for their forebears.

240
00:17:16,461 --> 00:17:22,700
But some modern scholars assert the Basilica's dome is almost 5 feet greater than the Pantheon's.

241
00:17:22,701 --> 00:17:24,205
But at 450 feet, St.

242
00:17:24,206 --> 00:17:29,320
Peter's was definitely much taller than any existing dome structure.

243
00:17:29,321 --> 00:17:30,021
St.

244
00:17:30,022 --> 00:17:36,320
Peter's was significant because it was a very large diameter dome, very tall, dome

245
00:17:36,800 --> 00:17:41,306
being the revolution of an arch in over 360 degrees. St.

246
00:17:41,307 --> 00:17:43,800
Peter's was a grand application

247
00:17:44,340 --> 00:17:51,340
of the marriage of that round shape of the dome with the square geometry of the church

248
00:17:51,341 --> 00:17:57,396
below. But the descendants of the Romans were not quite so masterful with St.

249
00:17:57,397 --> 00:17:58,340
Peter's. And

250
00:17:58,341 --> 00:18:01,840
For a few centuries, the dome began to crack,

251
00:18:01,841 --> 00:18:06,640
as the tremendous forces generated by the height of the dome pushed sideways.

252
00:18:06,641 --> 00:18:12,140
The original chain that had run around the perimeter of the dome

253
00:18:12,141 --> 00:18:14,340
was not sufficient to do the job.

254
00:18:14,341 --> 00:18:20,940
The dome was cracking, and they in fact needed to do additional strengthening work.

255
00:18:20,941 --> 00:18:26,740
The addition of three more chains to retain the horizontal force of the arch dome was sufficient.

256
00:18:26,741 --> 00:18:31,740
it has remained, damaged but stable for hundreds of years since.

257
00:18:32,740 --> 00:18:37,740
In 1965, the Houston Astrodome set new records for dome construction.

258
00:18:38,740 --> 00:18:47,740
At over 200 feet high and 710 feet wide, this giant leap in dome design was made possible with arches.

259
00:18:49,740 --> 00:18:56,740
The wonderful aspect of dome action is that they allow the use of arches to create very large volumes of interior

260
00:18:56,741 --> 00:18:57,441
space.

261
00:18:57,442 --> 00:19:02,060
300-yard football fields, 400 feet to center field, those are very long distances.

262
00:19:02,800 --> 00:19:09,700
The Astrodome was made with a series of pie-shaped wedges that are built in steel,

263
00:19:10,500 --> 00:19:14,880
and you could picture it in some ways as a series of arches that are leaning on each other.

264
00:19:15,220 --> 00:19:20,620
The point where these wedges meet is a giant metal ring, which acts as a keystone,

265
00:19:21,260 --> 00:19:25,160
completing the arch. The Houston Astrodome still stands today,

266
00:19:25,161 --> 00:19:28,700
But other domes took risks that ultimately didn't pay.

267
00:19:29,560 --> 00:19:31,460
Around the time the Astrodome was completed,

268
00:19:32,380 --> 00:19:34,780
another city began planning a similar project.

269
00:19:35,760 --> 00:19:39,520
The Kingdome is special because it's a pure compression dome.

270
00:19:39,800 --> 00:19:40,940
It was a concrete shell.

271
00:19:41,580 --> 00:19:43,280
It was extraordinarily thin.

272
00:19:44,160 --> 00:19:49,180
And the problem with it is that such a thin dome over such a long span

273
00:19:49,181 --> 00:19:52,480
can have difficulties with buckling.

274
00:19:52,481 --> 00:19:56,160
the great flaw of any compression structure.

275
00:19:57,920 --> 00:20:00,900
With its vertical spars and expansive interior,

276
00:20:01,800 --> 00:20:05,100
the King Dome served the citizens of Seattle for 24 years,

277
00:20:05,680 --> 00:20:10,780
but eventually closed because of water damage to its concrete roof.

278
00:20:11,500 --> 00:20:14,720
It was replaced with a new stadium that had a retractable cover,

279
00:20:15,100 --> 00:20:19,320
but for its time, it was one of the most daring concrete domes since the Pantheon.

280
00:20:19,321 --> 00:20:24,320
Domes continue to be used in modern construction to great effect.

281
00:20:24,321 --> 00:20:31,320
But the arch has found its way into other, sometimes surprising modern structures.

282
00:20:31,321 --> 00:20:36,320
And some arches literally do their jobs lying down.

283
00:20:40,320 --> 00:20:46,320
The Pantheon's dome is around 20 feet thick at the base, but only a quarter of that at its top,

284
00:20:46,321 --> 00:20:51,320
at its top, making the dome less heavy than it appears.

285
00:20:51,321 --> 00:20:56,960
The concrete near the top used volcanic pumice as an aggregate, maintaining strength while

286
00:20:56,961 --> 00:20:57,840
reducing weight.

287
00:21:16,320 --> 00:21:23,320
The power of the mighty Colorado River is harnessed in the American Southwest by nine

288
00:21:23,321 --> 00:21:24,049
main dams.

289
00:21:24,050 --> 00:21:30,320
Two of these enormous concrete structures are examples of the arch turned to a new and

290
00:21:30,321 --> 00:21:35,320
critical task. An arch becomes a dam when the arch

291
00:21:35,321 --> 00:21:41,320
is turned on its side and has the pressure of the water acting against it.

292
00:21:41,321 --> 00:21:46,320
In fact, many of these structures are just that, a simple arch.

293
00:21:46,321 --> 00:21:52,320
Arch dams are different than other dams because of the nature by which they resist the water forces.

294
00:21:52,321 --> 00:21:57,320
Most dams do so by the sheer weight or gravity.

295
00:21:57,321 --> 00:22:00,320
And arch dams do some of that because they have mass.

296
00:22:00,321 --> 00:22:07,320
But they're an arch tipped on its side and has arch action that thrusts the loads into the abutments,

297
00:22:07,321 --> 00:22:09,320
which are at the end of the arch.

298
00:22:09,321 --> 00:22:11,800
And those loads are resisted by the rock of the canyon.

299
00:22:12,300 --> 00:22:15,040
And they pretty much put the rock of the canyon into compression,

300
00:22:15,500 --> 00:22:19,820
which makes arch dams ideal for steep canyons, V-shaped, U-shaped canyons,

301
00:22:19,880 --> 00:22:23,440
with good strong rock on either side, which is where you'll find most arch dams.

302
00:22:26,700 --> 00:22:30,940
726 feet tall and up to 660 feet thick at its base,

303
00:22:31,480 --> 00:22:34,420
Hoover Dam was the largest concrete structure of its time.

304
00:22:34,421 --> 00:22:41,420
It required over 4.5 million cubic yards of concrete to build the massive arch.

305
00:22:41,421 --> 00:22:47,420
And it withstands the pressure of 9.2 trillion gallons of water in Lake Mead.

306
00:22:47,421 --> 00:22:54,420
As the water pressure comes from the depth, it's the hydrostatic pressure that comes from the depth of water that's supported.

307
00:22:54,421 --> 00:23:00,420
So, in fact, supporting a lake that's only one foot wide

308
00:23:00,421 --> 00:23:04,260
provides the exact same force that a lake that would be,

309
00:23:04,261 --> 00:23:05,860
you know, 100 miles wide.

310
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:09,720
As dam designs became increasingly daring,

311
00:23:10,580 --> 00:23:12,580
relying more upon the strength of arches,

312
00:23:13,600 --> 00:23:15,740
dams became thinner and more refined.

313
00:23:17,120 --> 00:23:20,020
One such structure resides not far from Hoover Dam.

314
00:23:21,620 --> 00:23:22,900
Glen Canyon's a beautiful arch.

315
00:23:22,901 --> 00:23:26,980
You can't deny that it's a very beautiful and magnificent engineering marvel.

316
00:23:27,380 --> 00:23:29,660
It's more of a thinner section. It's more of a true arch.

317
00:23:30,400 --> 00:23:36,460
With a thin concrete slab 710 feet high and 1560 feet long at the top,

318
00:23:37,340 --> 00:23:42,700
the dam relies on the shape and characteristics of the arch to resist the force of the water behind it,

319
00:23:43,180 --> 00:23:45,620
while using a minimum of material to do so.

320
00:23:47,160 --> 00:23:51,840
It's 25 feet thick at the top and over 300 feet thick at its base

321
00:23:51,841 --> 00:23:55,180
and is extremely strong for its relatively limited mass.

322
00:23:55,880 --> 00:23:58,420
The idea of the arch dam is to keep everything in compression

323
00:23:58,421 --> 00:24:01,480
and to minimize the bending that goes on in an arch.

324
00:24:02,540 --> 00:24:05,880
And if the dam can avoid tension, the enemy of any arch,

325
00:24:06,260 --> 00:24:09,760
and stay in compression, it can last for hundreds,

326
00:24:10,300 --> 00:24:11,900
perhaps thousands of years.

327
00:24:14,060 --> 00:24:16,720
Dams are clear examples of the strength of the arch.

328
00:24:17,440 --> 00:24:20,380
But less obvious, yet familiar to us all,

329
00:24:20,381 --> 00:24:24,600
are tunnels, which use the strength of the arch deep underground.

330
00:24:25,680 --> 00:24:33,500
Another example of arch action in which forces are redirected into compression is a tunnel.

331
00:24:34,520 --> 00:24:37,460
Many of us drive through tunnels on our daily commute.

332
00:24:38,020 --> 00:24:44,160
You'd have to ask yourself, where does the weight of all of the soil and rock above the tunnel go?

333
00:24:44,360 --> 00:24:47,780
It has to get redirected. It has to go around the tunnel.

334
00:24:47,781 --> 00:24:51,160
and it goes around the tunnel because of arch action.

335
00:24:52,820 --> 00:24:55,320
While the Romans built small arch tunnels,

336
00:24:55,740 --> 00:24:59,420
the craft of modern tunneling really started in England in 1825

337
00:24:59,421 --> 00:25:02,820
when Mark Brunel took on the great challenge

338
00:25:02,821 --> 00:25:04,440
of digging beneath the River Thames.

339
00:25:06,900 --> 00:25:09,820
In the case of Brunel's tunnel under the Thames,

340
00:25:10,180 --> 00:25:12,920
that if he'd built just one large tunnel, one large opening,

341
00:25:13,020 --> 00:25:16,020
he was concerned about the loading coming from the sides

342
00:25:16,021 --> 00:25:16,800
failing the tunnel.

343
00:25:16,801 --> 00:25:24,880
And so what he did was he introduced a central pier with two kind of arches that were bearing on that central column.

344
00:25:25,780 --> 00:25:31,360
He was concerned about the size of the forces, so he cut the span in half by adding a central wall.

345
00:25:32,440 --> 00:25:35,740
Brunel's innovative tunnel paved the way for something much bigger.

346
00:25:37,540 --> 00:25:44,540
Since before Brunel's time, engineers had dreamt of a tunnel under the English Channel, connecting Britain with France.

347
00:25:44,541 --> 00:25:49,540
This dream became reality in 1994.

348
00:25:52,540 --> 00:25:57,540
An arched cylinder was the perfect answer for the 31-mile run under the English Channel,

349
00:25:57,541 --> 00:26:00,540
up to 530 feet under the water's surface.

350
00:26:01,540 --> 00:26:04,540
These tunnels are 24 feet wide

351
00:26:05,540 --> 00:26:08,540
and are formed of a series of continuous arches,

352
00:26:08,541 --> 00:26:11,540
proposed to each other at an infinite number of points.

353
00:26:11,541 --> 00:26:21,540
In this application of the arch, the triple tubes are lined with a combination of concrete and iron compression rings up to 23 inches thick.

354
00:26:21,541 --> 00:26:28,540
A cylindrical tunnel is an example of almost perfect compression, the best environment for an arched form.

355
00:26:28,541 --> 00:26:37,540
Just as the arch provides us access under the sea, so it provides passage over the sea.

356
00:26:37,541 --> 00:26:41,540
Without the arch, bridge building as we know it

357
00:26:41,541 --> 00:26:42,540
would be impossible.

358
00:26:46,540 --> 00:26:49,540
When built, the Channel Tunnel was the most expensive

359
00:26:49,541 --> 00:26:53,540
construction project ever conceived, with a price tag

360
00:26:53,541 --> 00:26:55,540
of $21 billion.

361
00:27:07,540 --> 00:27:17,140
The wider the valley, the longer the bridge.

362
00:27:17,141 --> 00:27:22,780
And arched structures are often the best solution to that age-old question, how to get to the

363
00:27:22,781 --> 00:27:25,680
other side.

364
00:27:25,681 --> 00:27:29,440
Once again, the Romans got there first.

365
00:27:29,441 --> 00:27:31,460
Visit Rome today.

366
00:27:31,461 --> 00:27:39,700
And it comes as a surprise to most visitors that many of the bridges in Rome are 2,000

367
00:27:39,701 --> 00:27:40,700
years old.

368
00:27:40,701 --> 00:27:46,300
Among the most beautiful is the Ponte Fabrico, which is a wonderful example of Roman arched

369
00:27:46,301 --> 00:27:47,300
design.

370
00:27:47,301 --> 00:27:53,000
One of the first arched bridges in the world, the Ponte Fabricio allowed Roman soldiers

371
00:27:53,001 --> 00:27:58,580
to cross from the banks of the Tiber River to an island in its center, 200 feet away.

372
00:27:58,581 --> 00:28:04,540
The Romans, being excellent engineers and innovators, discovered through accident first

373
00:28:04,541 --> 00:28:09,680
and then through continued experiment that the concept of the arch could be used to great

374
00:28:09,681 --> 00:28:10,680
advantage.

375
00:28:10,681 --> 00:28:15,480
The Ponte Fabricio was the first recorded attempt by the Romans to use an arch and a

376
00:28:15,481 --> 00:28:17,640
bridge.

377
00:28:17,641 --> 00:28:21,900
It continues to serve pedestrians in the 21st century.

378
00:28:21,901 --> 00:28:26,680
Since its completion, engineers have built tens of thousands of arch-supported bridges

379
00:28:26,681 --> 00:28:28,275
throughout the world.

380
00:28:28,276 --> 00:28:33,440
In the 18th century, materials such as iron became viable for bridge

381
00:28:33,441 --> 00:28:34,400
construction.

382
00:28:34,401 --> 00:28:40,600
The Colbrookdale Bridge in England is an example of an early wrought iron structure.

383
00:28:42,880 --> 00:28:50,820
Completed in 1779 after only three months of assembly, it spanned 100 feet and was the first

384
00:28:50,821 --> 00:28:52,480
all-iron arched bridge.

385
00:28:53,820 --> 00:28:57,160
This beautiful structure was born of the Industrial Revolution

386
00:28:57,161 --> 00:29:00,180
and allowed for a light, airy appearance

387
00:29:00,181 --> 00:29:02,920
that greatly excited bridge designers worldwide.

388
00:29:06,960 --> 00:29:11,360
In 1880, Gustave Eiffel, later of tower fame,

389
00:29:11,860 --> 00:29:15,340
began construction on one of the first metal arch bridges in France.

390
00:29:16,940 --> 00:29:19,200
Gustave Eiffel was a bridge engineer,

391
00:29:19,201 --> 00:29:24,020
and he built probably the most elegant iron arch bridge ever built,

392
00:29:24,380 --> 00:29:27,300
which is the Garbi Viaduct in central France.

393
00:29:27,580 --> 00:29:32,140
You had to have a long span and you had to have it be stable side to side.

394
00:29:32,820 --> 00:29:37,040
So the Garbi Viaduct solved this by having a beautiful crescent-shaped arch,

395
00:29:37,600 --> 00:29:40,720
but then Eiffel flared out the base of the arch

396
00:29:40,721 --> 00:29:45,240
in the same way that he flared the legs of his tower 20 years later

397
00:29:45,241 --> 00:29:50,440
in order to reduce the loading and make it more stable under the lateral wind load.

398
00:29:51,380 --> 00:29:56,180
Completed in 1884, the viaduct spans over 1,850 feet

399
00:29:56,181 --> 00:29:59,260
and stands 400 feet above the river.

400
00:30:00,900 --> 00:30:04,840
It would inspire many bridge builders to lend an artistic flair to their work.

401
00:30:06,660 --> 00:30:10,040
The arch even finds its way into the longest bridges in the world,

402
00:30:11,040 --> 00:30:11,920
suspension bridges,

403
00:30:11,921 --> 00:30:18,920
San Francisco's Golden Gate is a classic example.

404
00:30:18,921 --> 00:30:24,920
The building of the Golden Gate was a landmark in civil engineering.

405
00:30:24,921 --> 00:30:34,920
It was completed with fewer injuries than any previous bridge construction, and would span one of the most treacherous waterways in the world.

406
00:30:34,921 --> 00:30:40,600
By the time it was completed, the use of an inverse arch as a suspension element was well established.

407
00:30:41,960 --> 00:30:45,980
A suspension bridge is exactly the opposite of an arch bridge.

408
00:30:45,981 --> 00:30:51,680
If we think back to the hanging chain, the loads on a suspension bridge are hanging down,

409
00:30:52,040 --> 00:30:58,560
suspending the roadway from a single cable or sometimes a few cables that are parabolic in shape.

410
00:30:58,760 --> 00:30:59,880
And they're pure tension.

411
00:30:59,881 --> 00:31:10,180
So if you wanted to turn a suspension bridge into an arch bridge, you would have to freeze those cables, turn them upside down, and put them under the roadway.

412
00:31:10,980 --> 00:31:17,700
The roadway of a suspension bridge often has an upward camber in the middle, a slight arching effect.

413
00:31:18,140 --> 00:31:24,380
And that's largely aesthetic, that it somehow is more comforting to the eye to see a slight rise in the roadway.

414
00:31:24,381 --> 00:31:31,960
In addition to the huge cables that hold the bridge up, acting in tension, many suspension

415
00:31:31,961 --> 00:31:37,620
bridges are still supported in part by the traditional compression arch.

416
00:31:37,621 --> 00:31:43,460
On the Golden Gate's south end, a large metal arch supports the roadbed as it clears historic

417
00:31:43,461 --> 00:31:47,880
Fort Point.

418
00:31:47,881 --> 00:31:52,400
Arch-supported bridges come in many forms and varieties.

419
00:31:52,401 --> 00:31:55,480
such as the Bixby Bridge on California's Pacific Coast Highway,

420
00:31:56,240 --> 00:31:58,360
use just one long, shallow arch.

421
00:31:59,720 --> 00:32:02,780
This stunning span, completed in 1932,

422
00:32:03,260 --> 00:32:07,160
is 714 feet long and stands over 240 feet

423
00:32:07,161 --> 00:32:09,260
above the crashing waves near Big Sur.

424
00:32:11,220 --> 00:32:13,240
The roadbed is connected to the arch

425
00:32:13,241 --> 00:32:16,180
with numerous spandrel columns, or vertical braces.

426
00:32:17,300 --> 00:32:20,400
Bixby Creek Bridge was built in the 1930s,

427
00:32:20,401 --> 00:32:24,080
And initially, when the bridge was put out to bid,

428
00:32:24,580 --> 00:32:29,320
the contractor had the option of constructing a steel arch as opposed to concrete arches,

429
00:32:29,321 --> 00:32:33,700
the thought being that the steel arches would be much easier to construct

430
00:32:33,701 --> 00:32:35,440
because they could be constructed from the roadway.

431
00:32:35,960 --> 00:32:39,340
They wouldn't be impacted by the waves or the creek down below.

432
00:32:40,220 --> 00:32:44,960
However, the contractor decided that it would be less expensive to construct the bridge

433
00:32:44,961 --> 00:32:46,880
using the concrete arch structure.

434
00:32:46,881 --> 00:32:51,880
One advantage of concrete in such a bridge is its incredible strength.

435
00:32:51,881 --> 00:32:55,880
An arch structure is primarily a compression element.

436
00:32:55,881 --> 00:33:01,880
The vertical load path of the bridge will go from the T-beam deck into each of the columns,

437
00:33:01,881 --> 00:33:03,880
and then from the columns into the arch.

438
00:33:03,881 --> 00:33:08,880
The arch will then take that load and distribute it to the two towers on either side of the bridge.

439
00:33:08,881 --> 00:33:11,880
That's the beauty of an arch.

440
00:33:11,881 --> 00:33:15,880
Other spans have arches above the roadway.

441
00:33:15,881 --> 00:33:19,320
such as the Desmond Bridge in Long Beach, California.

442
00:33:21,160 --> 00:33:23,640
But this bridge is rapidly deteriorating

443
00:33:23,641 --> 00:33:27,300
and will soon be replaced with a design pioneered

444
00:33:27,301 --> 00:33:29,680
by the Natchez Trace Bridge in Tennessee.

445
00:33:31,260 --> 00:33:35,480
This bridge is the first precast concrete segmental arch bridge

446
00:33:35,481 --> 00:33:38,440
in the United States with 582 foot span.

447
00:33:38,441 --> 00:33:42,200
The bridge is unique because traditional arch structures

448
00:33:42,201 --> 00:33:48,200
carry the load of the deck through a series of vertical members,

449
00:33:48,360 --> 00:33:51,580
which are called spandrels, that are then connected to the arch.

450
00:33:52,500 --> 00:33:58,160
In this case, the vision was to open up the arch and eliminate the spandrels.

451
00:33:58,860 --> 00:34:05,180
That way, the load of the deck is all carried at the crown of the arch.

452
00:34:05,181 --> 00:34:09,480
The arch is, in fact, independent from the superstructure.

453
00:34:09,481 --> 00:34:20,065
The superstructure sits on bearings on top of the arch, and it's the load that is transmitted from the superstructure, the top deck, into the crown of the arch, which

454
00:34:20,066 --> 00:34:21,340
transmits that load.

455
00:34:22,720 --> 00:34:27,360
The Natchez Trace Bridge represents the state-of-the-art in arched bridge construction.

456
00:34:28,620 --> 00:34:33,820
And since its completion in 1994, it has become an emblem of modern bridge design.

457
00:34:33,821 --> 00:34:45,060
In the past, arches have served us in buildings, dams, tunnels, and bridges, and they will continue to serve us in our ever-expanding future.

458
00:34:49,860 --> 00:34:57,760
The longest concrete and masonry arch-supported bridge in the world is the Rockville Bridge in Pennsylvania, completed in 1902.

459
00:34:57,761 --> 00:35:06,600
It has 48 arches in its 3,800-foot span and was built primarily by immigrant Italian stonemasons and Irish laborers.

460
00:35:18,060 --> 00:35:20,360
Not all arches are structural.

461
00:35:20,361 --> 00:35:27,520
The graceful form of the arch and the dome has captivated onlookers and users for thousands

462
00:35:27,521 --> 00:35:28,520
of years.

463
00:35:28,521 --> 00:35:33,900
I think the arch is a consistent form that always recurs, regardless of material and

464
00:35:33,901 --> 00:35:34,900
culture.

465
00:35:34,901 --> 00:35:37,920
You can see it in almost every architecture in some form or another.

466
00:35:37,921 --> 00:35:43,080
It's very interesting to see its transformation in contemporary times into new materials and

467
00:35:43,081 --> 00:35:44,080
forms.

468
00:35:44,081 --> 00:35:49,860
We're surrounded by arches that are primarily ornamental in function.

469
00:35:49,861 --> 00:35:55,480
are mere add-ons to contemporary architecture, while others are integral parts of an overall

